  "id": "DrugBank_DB01626 - MONDO_5044",
  "verbalization": "Pargyline is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypertensive Disorder through its role as an indication for Hypertension, which in turn links to Hypertensive Disorder via multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Pargyline may exert a therapeutic effect on hypertension by influencing the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), as indicated by its association with Rilmenidine and Zofenopril, both of which are angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors. Similarly, its association with Labetalol, a beta-blocker, implies a potential modulation of cardiovascular function. The final scores across the three paths, which are consistent and above a threshold of 0.55, suggest a plausible mechanistic link between Pargyline and Hypertensive Disorder, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Pargyline to contribute to the management of hypertension, which is a key driver of Hypertensive Disorder. The evidence supports a biologically plausible mechanism, but further studies are needed to confirm the direct relationship and to assess the clinical relevance of this repurposing.",
